# The warren tree PAX carries as /usr/bin/warren in the dev image (px142;
# tools/mkwarren builds it).
#
# warren is the terminal multiplexer in wolf (wolffe-lang/warren): one
# server per user holds every pseudo-terminal and repaints its clients from
# its own screens; a client attaches over a Unix-domain socket and can detach
# and come back. Behind the prefix (Ctrl-Space) a session splits into panes
# and holds tabs. Its emulator is held, screen for screen, to a pinned
# reference emulator run as a binary; its session rows run on Linux, macOS
# and PAX under QEMU. Its clean room is the documents its README names; PAX
# reads no more of its tree than building it needs.
#
# warren trunk 9ef76d7 (2026-11-15): one pane, detach and attach, a second
# client, `warren new`, the server outliving its client, behind the prefix
# splits and tabs (warren#14), a session that holds at PAX's console, where
# the terminal's size reads 0 0 (warren#19, for warren#18), and a display's
# retries bounded by the rate hosted runners measured. Pinned in its own
# wolf-toolchain.toml at wolf 0.2.40 and wolf-std c339a471 (newer than the
# std that archive bundles: the one wait, the one-send write and peer
# credentials its server is built on). tools/mkwarren builds it with THAT
# toolchain and std, twice from fresh checkouts, refusing two digests, and
# ships the stripped copy. A --latest build resolves it like the other trees.
WARREN_REPO=https://github.com/wolffe-lang/warren
WARREN_REV=9ef76d73bff5ffd3da2615d98494cc45576338fb
